WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.453
Check-then-act separates an observation
from the action that relies on it.

2
00:00:04.453 --> 00:00:07.290
The inventory example reads a quantity,
tests whether

3
00:00:07.290 --> 00:00:10.641
it is positive, and later writes a
calculated replacement.

4
00:00:10.641 --> 00:00:12.704
Each individual call can succeed while

5
00:00:12.704 --> 00:00:14.939
the overall business operation is
incorrect.

6
00:00:14.939 --> 00:00:17.775
With one unit available, two requests can
both

7
00:00:17.775 --> 00:00:20.353
read one and both decide to sell.

8
00:00:20.353 --> 00:00:23.963
Each then writes zero based on its own
earlier read.

9
00:00:23.963 --> 00:00:26.799
The final quantity looks plausible, yet
two requests

10
00:00:26.799 --> 00:00:29.377
may have received success for one unit.

11
00:00:29.377 --> 00:00:32.300
This is oversubscription combined with a
lost decrement.

12
00:00:32.300 --> 00:00:35.394
Replacing the assignment with an
unconditional atomic decrement avoids

13
00:00:35.394 --> 00:00:38.745
that lost update, but could drive quantity
below zero.

14
00:00:38.745 --> 00:00:42.699
The availability condition must be part of
the protected transition too.

15
00:00:42.699 --> 00:00:44.933
Lost updates are broader than inventory.

16
00:00:44.933 --> 00:00:47.770
Two writers can read the same document
version,

17
00:00:47.770 --> 00:00:50.778
change different fields, and write back
whole objects.

18
00:00:50.778 --> 00:00:54.044
The later replacement can erase the
earlier writer's change.

19
00:00:54.044 --> 00:00:56.966
Stale browser forms, background jobs, and
administrative updates

20
00:00:56.966 --> 00:01:00.317
all participate, even when requests reach
different service replicas.

21
00:01:00.317 --> 00:01:02.380
Sequential happy-path tests rarely reveal
this

22
00:01:02.380 --> 00:01:04.958
because each writer observes the previous
result.

23
00:01:04.958 --> 00:01:07.021
Adding another application-level check
merely introduces

24
00:01:07.021 --> 00:01:09.256
another observation that can become stale.

25
00:01:09.256 --> 00:01:12.350
An in-memory flag is also insufficient
when another process

26
00:01:12.350 --> 00:01:15.616
can reach the authoritative record without
consulting that flag.

27
00:01:15.616 --> 00:01:18.366
Keeping database calls adjacent in
TypeScript does not

28
00:01:18.366 --> 00:01:20.944
combine them into one atomic storage
action.

29
00:01:20.944 --> 00:01:24.382
Review each read-modify-write sequence by
identifying what could change after

30
00:01:24.382 --> 00:01:27.648
the read and which outcome would violate
the invariant.

31
00:01:27.648 --> 00:01:29.711
Prefer expressing the condition and
mutation

32
00:01:29.711 --> 00:01:32.289
together when the storage system supports
it.

33
00:01:32.289 --> 00:01:35.125
When several records or decisions must be
protected,

34
00:01:35.125 --> 00:01:38.047
use a transaction with appropriate
isolation or locking.

35
00:01:38.047 --> 00:01:40.883
When an earlier user edit must be
preserved,

36
00:01:40.883 --> 00:01:43.805
a version precondition may be the right
control.

37
00:01:43.805 --> 00:01:46.642
Choose the mechanism for the invariant,
rather than

38
00:01:46.642 --> 00:01:49.220
treating every overlap as the same
problem.

